I have an Eloquent model which has a related model:
```public function option() {
    return $this->hasOne('RepairOption', 'repair_item_id');
}
public function setOptionArrayAttribute($values)
{
    $this->option->update($values);
}
```
When I create the model, it does not necessarily have a related model. When I update it, I might add an option, or not.
So I need to check if the related model exists, to either update it, or create it, respectively:
```$model = RepairItem::find($id);
if (Input::has('option')) {
    if () {
        $option = new RepairOption(Input::get('option'));
        $option->repairItem()->associate($model);
        $option->save();
        $model->fill(Input::except('option');
    } else {
       $model->update(Input::all());
    }
};
```
Where `````` is the code I am looking for.

text:
`if ($model->option)` — a `hasOne` relation returns `null` when nothing is related, so a truthy check is all you need.

A few notes on your snippet:

- `$this->option->update($values)` in the mutator will throw if `option` is null. Use `$this->option()->updateOrCreate([], $values)` instead — it handles both cases and removes the branching entirely.
- `Input::except('option')` is missing its closing paren, and `Input::all()` would include the `option` key, which you probably don't want passed to `fill`.
- `updateOrCreate` on the relation also sets the foreign key for you, so you can drop the manual `associate` + `save`.

Simpler version:

```php
$model = RepairItem::find($id);
$model->fill(Input::except('option'));

if (Input::has('option')) {
    $model->option()->updateOrCreate([], Input::get('option'));
}

$model->save();
```

If you prefer the explicit check, `if ($model->option === null)` is clearer than a bare truthy test.

