Use `System.Net.Mail` (built-in) or `MailKit` (better for modern auth). Here's the built-in approach:

```csharp
using System.Net;
using System.Net.Mail;

var message = new MailMessage
{
    From = new MailAddress("you@example.com"),
    Subject = "Hello",
    Body = "<h1>Hi</h1><p>This is <b>HTML</b>.</p>",
    IsBodyHtml = true
};
message.To.Add("them@example.com");

using var client = new SmtpClient("smtp.example.com", 587)
{
    Credentials = new NetworkCredential("you@example.com", "password"),
    EnableSsl = true
};
client.Send(message);
```

The key line is `IsBodyHtml = true`. Without it, the HTML shows as literal text.

Two things worth knowing:

- `SmtpClient` is marked obsolete for new development by Microsoft. It still works, but `MailKit` is the recommended choice — better TLS, OAuth2 support, and actively maintained.
- Many providers (Gmail, Outlook) now require app passwords or OAuth2, not your normal login password.

For anything beyond a quick script, I'd use MailKit. Want the MailKit version?

In Rust, `for (key, value) in &map` is the usual choice — it borrows the map and gives you `(&K, &V)` pairs.

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;

let map: HashMap<String, i32> = HashMap::new();

for (key, value) in &map {
    println!("{key}: {value}");
}
```

Variants depending on what you need:

- `&map` — borrow, keys and values by reference (most common).
- `&mut map` — mutable references, if you need to change values in place.
- `map` — consumes the map, giving owned keys and values.
- `map.keys()` or `map.values()` — when you only need one side.
- `map.iter()` — same as `&map`, explicit form.

One caveat: iteration order is arbitrary and changes between runs, since `HashMap` uses a randomized hasher. If you need a stable order, collect into a `Vec` and sort, or use `BTreeMap`.

(Assuming Rust — say the word if you meant a different language's `HashMap`.)

