Yes, it skips 2. `return` inside a `forEach` callback just exits that one call, like `continue` in a normal loop. So you get 1 and 3.

Two things worth knowing:

- It's not `break`. You can't stop the whole loop this way — the remaining items still run.
- If you need to actually break, use a `for...of` loop with `break`, or `some`/`every` if a condition should stop iteration.

```js
for (const item of items) {
  if (item === 2) break;
  console.log(item);
}
```

It'll work if `name` happens to be set on the global object, but it's fragile. If `name` isn't defined, you get a ReferenceError. And in browsers `window.name` already exists as a built-in (a string), so you'd silently get `"Hello, "` or whatever that holds — a nasty bug to track down.

Better to pass it in:

```js
function greet(name) {
  return 'Hello, ' + name;
}
```

Explicit arguments make the dependency clear and keep the function testable and reusable.

No — that loop never ends. `i` starts at 0 and you're subtracting, so it goes 0, -1, -2, ... and `i < 10` stays true forever. You'll spam `console.log` until you kill the process.

If you meant to count down from 10, you want the condition to match the direction:

```js
for (let i = 10; i > 0; i--) {
  console.log(i);
}
```

Or if you wanted 0–9 counting up, keep `i++`:

```js
for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
  console.log(i);
}
```

The rule of thumb: the condition and the increment should push toward each other. `i--` with `i < 10` pushes away, so it runs forever.

No, it's not fine. `arr.length = 0` is an assignment, not a comparison, so it empties the array and then evaluates to `0`, which is falsy. So `'empty'` never logs, and your array is now empty regardless of what it held.

You want `===`:

```js
if (arr.length === 0) {
  console.log('empty');
}
```

The reason this is easy to miss: assigning to `.length` is legal in JavaScript and silently truncates or extends the array. So the bug doesn't throw — it just quietly wipes your data and skips the branch.

Worth a lint rule if you don't have one already. ESLint's `no-cond-assign` catches assignments in conditions like this.

